{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And it shall be righteousness for us, etc.</b> One needs to understand the intent in the term <b>צְדָקָה</b>, and Ramban explained that it refers back to the reward for the commandments, for it will be in the manner of צְדָקָה from HaShem — since we are servants and obligated to serve, and we have no legal claim to reward — up to here [is his words], but the verse is not well-settled according to his words, may his memory be for blessing. Further, one needs to understand the intent of His saying <b>before HaShem, as He has commanded us</b>, etc." "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent is that there are two levels in the service of HaShem. The first is service from fear, and this is an obligation upon all created beings, for every servant fears his master, as it says (Malachi 1:6): ״אִם אֲדוֹנִים אָנִי אַיֵּה מוֹרָאִי״, and this is what the verse says (Deuteronomy 10:12): ״מָה ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ שׁוֹאֵל מֵעִמָּךְ כִּי אִם לְיִרְאָה״. The second is service from love, and it is a great and mighty commandment, but its obligation does not stem from the fact that a person is a servant — that it be required of him to love; it suffices that he not hate and that he serve as needed. And this is the thing a servant is obligated to his master for, and correspondingly the master is obligated to feed him and sustain him according to the master's ability. Corresponding to the obligation that falls upon him as a servant, the verse stated earlier <b>וַיְצַוֵּנוּ ה׳ לְיִרְאָה אֶת ה׳</b>, and concluded by saying <b>לְטוֹב לָנוּ</b> etc. <b>לְחַיּוֹתֵנוּ</b> etc. — meaning, the commandment of HaShem comes as an obligation of fear, according to the judgment of a servant to his king, and also upon HaShem is [the duty] to do good, according to the king's ability, to provide sustenance and nourishment לְחַיּוֹתֵנוּ, for a master is obligated in the sustenance of his servant." "And His saying <b>כְּהַיּוֹם הַזֶּה</b> — meaning: in the same order that He does this day, that He feeds and sustains, bringing down the manna, etc., and clothes them, as they said, may their memory be for blessing (Deuteronomy Rabbah 7), that their garments grew with them like a snail and the clouds pressed their garments. And corresponding to service from love, He said this — it is not under the servant's obligation but in the manner of <b>צְדָקָה תִּהְיֶה לָנוּ כִּי נִשְׁמֹר</b> etc. <b>לִפְנֵי ה׳</b>. He recorded in this the ultimate goal of love, following their words, may their memory be for blessing (Hovot ha-Levavot, Gate of the Love of HaShem, ch. 1), and this is his language: ״Love is the soul's consummation and its inclination toward the Creator, and this is the soul's cleaving before the sweet light, which is HaShem our Elohim.״" "And His saying <b>כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוָּנוּ</b> — meaning: not for the sake of any ulterior motive, but rather to perform the commandment of HaShem, as it says (Psalms 40:9): ״לַעֲשׂוֹת רְצוֹנְךָ אֱלֹהַי חָפַצְתִּי״ — for this is performing the commandment in complete love. And see what I explained in parashat Aḥarei Mot at the verse (Leviticus 18:2) ״כְּמַעֲשֵׂה אֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ etc. And through this matter a person will be justified and may hope for reward even according to strict law. And although in truth we are obligated to pay reward to Elohim blessed be He, for He implanted in us the pleasantness, the delight, the sweetness of His love in our heart — sweet and pleasant beyond limit — still a person does receive reward for his exertion in having attained a taste of the [true] life." "<b>Further,</b> the verse hints at a hidden allusion: that through the medium of observing the commandments, our portion will be the Shekhinah, may He be blessed, which is called צְדָקָה (Tikkunei Zohar 21). And understand this."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:51.922640199Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}